 men before the competitions, but the course was modified slightly. However, several women withdrew on the morning of the event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The favorite was American " [:a {:href "/athletes/128763"} "Jamie Anderson"] ", who had won eight medals in the last nine years at the Winter X-Games, including four gold medals in 2007-08 and 2012-13. In 2014 she had won silver at the X-Games, trailing Norwegian " [:a {:href "/athletes/128228"} "Silje Norendal"] ". Anderson lived up to expectations, winning the gold medal with her second run of the final, edging out Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/127701"} "Enni Rukajärvi"] ", the 2011 World Champion. The bronze medal went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/127794"} "Jenny Jones"] ", to win Great Britain’s first ever Winter Olympic medal on snow."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "18"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/128763"} "Jamie Anderson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "93.50 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "95.25 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "8"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/127701"} "Enni Rukajärvi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "79.00 (4 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "92.50 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/127794"} "Jenny Jones"]] [:td {} [:a {:h